To create a customized sleep schedule for dogs and cats, we can use the crontab command in Linux. Crontab is a time-based job scheduler in Unix-like operating systems that allows users to schedule jobs (commands or scripts) to run at a specific time or interval.

Here's an example of how to set up a customized sleep schedule for a dog using the crontab command:

```
crontab -e
```

This command will open the crontab file in your default text editor. Add the following line to the file:

```
0 22 * * * /path/to/dog_sleep.sh
```

This line will run the `dog_sleep.sh` script at 10:00 PM every day. You can modify the time and the script path to suit your needs.

Here's an example of how to set up a customized sleep schedule for a cat using the crontab command:

```
